# BLOCKED - not a loadable rule. The .draft extension keeps it out of the
# validator's glob deliberately (schema section 1). Move it to rules/atomic/
# and drop .draft only when the blocker below is cleared.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rule 3 of the six the plan requires: encoded / download PowerShell.
#
# THE BLOCKER: no field on Event carries the script text.
#
# The 4104 parser is deliberate about this, and its reasoning is sound:
#
#     # ScriptBlockText is deliberately NOT kept: a single block can run to
#     # tens of kilobytes, details is carried in every timeline row, and the
#     # text is recoverable from the source record via source_locator.
#     keep = ("ScriptBlockId", "MessageNumber", "MessageTotal", "Path")
#         -- dfir_triage_pipeline/parsers/evtx_windows.py
#
# The consequence is that a rule cannot see what the script said. For a
# script_block event: path is None, action is the fixed string "powershell
# script block logged", and details holds only the fragment identifiers and an
# optional source Path. Every condition this rule needs would read a field that
# does not exist, and CD-012 correctly rejects it at load time.
#
# It is not written against an undeclared key and left to fail quietly. That is
# the exact failure mode the schema exists to prevent, so it is parked here
# instead, visible and inert.
#
# WHAT WOULD UNBLOCK IT, in preference order. This is Person B's call - the
# parser and the details schema are both his.
#
#   1. A bounded text key. Declare `script_block_text` capped at a stated byte
#      count, plus `script_block_text_bytes` carrying the FULL length and
#      `script_block_truncated` when the cap was hit. The cap answers the size
#      objection; the two companions keep the truncation honest.
#
#      This needs one schema clause on Person A's side before it is usable, and
#      the clause matters more than the key: a truncated field may be read by
#      POSITIVE operators only. `not_contains` against a field that holds the
#      first N bytes of a 40 KB script asserts something the evidence does not
#      support, and would read as a clean negative. rules/schema.md will reject
#      negative operators against a declared-truncated key in 1.2.0 if this
#      option is taken.
#
#   2. Parser-side indicator flags - `script_encoded`, `script_downloads` - as
#      booleans in details. Cheaper and smaller, but it moves detection logic
#      into the parser, which is the rule layer's job, and it makes the
#      indicator untunable without a code change. Not preferred.
#
#   3. Leave it. Record in Week 4's limitations that the encoded-PowerShell
#      indicator was specified and could not be evaluated on this corpus,
#      naming the reason. Legitimate, and better than a rule that appears to
#      work and matches nothing.
#
# The scenario does not depend on this either way: Scenario 1's delivery was
# HTTP through Edge, not PowerShell, so nothing in the acceptance gate turns on
# it. It is a required indicator, not a required detection.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The rule as it would be written under option 1, for review:

schema_version: "1.2.0"
id: R-ATOMIC-0003
type: atomic
title: PowerShell script block with an encoded command or an in-memory download
enabled: true

description: >
  Fires on an EID 4104 script block whose text carries a base64-encoded command
  or an in-memory download primitive. Claims that the block was logged with
  that content and names the record. Because the text field is capped, a
  negative result means "not found in the recorded portion", never "absent" -
  which is why no condition here uses a negative operator.

severity: high
weight: 65

attack:
  technique: T1059.001
  name: Command and Scripting Interpreter - PowerShell

select:
  event_type: [script_block]
  source_artifact: [evtx.powershell]

conditions:
  all:
    - field: event_type
      op: equals
      value: script_block
    - field: details.script_block_text
      op: present
  any:
    - field: details.script_block_text
      op: matches
      value: '(?i)\s-(enc|encodedcommand|e|ec)\s+[A-Za-z0-9+/=]{40,}'
    - field: details.script_block_text
      op: matches
      value: '(?i)frombase64string'
    - field: details.script_block_text
      op: matches
      value: '(?i)(net\.)?webclient|downloadstring|downloadfile|invoke-webrequest|\biwr\b|start-bitstransfer'
    - field: details.script_block_text
      op: matches
      value: '(?i)invoke-expression|\biex\b'

expected:
  clean_a: unmeasured
  clean_b: unmeasured
  s1_v2: unmeasured
  note: >
    Cannot be measured until the blocker is cleared. Person A's channel
    inventory should be consulted first: if collection_s1_v2 holds no 4104
    records at all, then this rule's expected value is 0 everywhere for a
    second and independent reason, and option 3 above becomes the sensible
    answer rather than the resigned one.

notes: >
  MessageNumber and MessageTotal are carried by the parser precisely so a
  consumer can tell a whole script from one third of one. A finding from this
  rule must report both, because a match in fragment 2 of 5 says the script
  contained the indicator, not that this fragment is the script.
